## Baseline Model
The original LSTM model follows the official Keras time-series forecasting example using the Jena Climate dataset. It uses:
A single LSTM layer
Fixed sequence length (historical window)
Default batch size and learning rate
Basic preprocessing and scaling
One-step forecasting output

## Increased Model Depth (Stacked LSTM)
Modified from single LSTM → multiple LSTM layers
Allows the model to learn:
Low-level temporal patterns (first layer)
Higher-level trends (deeper layers)
Impact:
Better ability to capture long-term dependencies
Improved forecasting stability for complex weather patterns



The improvements made in Part C+B demonstrate how relatively simple modifications can significantly enhance time-series forecasting performance. By stacking LSTM layers, increasing the input sequence length, and tuning hyperparameters, the model becomes more capable of learning both short-term fluctuations and long-term temporal dependencies in weather data.

These changes resulted in more stable training and better generalization compared to the baseline model. The experiment highlights that effective time-series modeling is not only about choosing the right architecture but also about carefully designing the input data and training configuration.

Overall, the LSTM model proved to be flexible and powerful for forecasting tasks, and the improvements provided valuable insight into how deep learning models can be optimized for real-world sequential data problems.
